{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Foundations of Interactive Multimedia Learning","title":"Design Rationale as Evidence","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting design rationale as evidence to the design-rationale log entry with claim, evidence, warrant, rejected alternatives, and planned check, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-01","statement":"Distinguish a description of a design (what it contains) from a rationale (why, with alternatives weighed)"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-02","statement":"Structure a rationale entry as claim, evidence, warrant, and rejected alternatives"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning","ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/01/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_ai_boundary"],"ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_calibration"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Design Rationale as Evidence\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Design Rationale As Evidence, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/01-07-design-rationale-as-evidence.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Design Rationale as Evidence: Design Rationale As Evidence, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA finished prototype shows what you decided; it does not show why, what you rejected, or what you traded away. Six months later, when a stakeholder asks \"why is Screen 4 a branching scenario and not a quiz?\", a polished screen cannot answer, but a design rationale can. This section treats rationale as the load-bearing evidence of design competence. Drawing on design-rationale traditions (the question-option-criteria approach and Toulmin's structure of an argument), it asks you to record not just the decision but the *argument* for it: the claim, the data that grounds it, the warrant connecting them, and the alternatives you weighed and discarded. Your rationale log is the artifact that makes your reasoning auditable and your design revisable by someone other than you.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a description of a design (what it contains) from a rationale (why, with alternatives weighed).\n- Structure a rationale entry as claim, evidence, warrant, and rejected alternatives.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA rationale log entry reads: \"Screen 4 has a branching scenario with three choices, a progress bar, and a Submit button. The colors match our brand.\" Every word is true and none of it is rationale. It catalogs what the screen contains and says nothing about why a branching scenario rather than a quiz, what evidence motivated it, or what was considered and rejected. A reviewer learns the screen's contents but cannot evaluate, challenge, or improve a single decision. The case is not \"describe the screen better.\" It is \"turn the description into an argument a skeptic could attack.\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA design rationale is an argument, and arguments have parts. Borrowing Toulmin's structure, a strong rationale entry exposes:\n\n- **Claim:** the decision (use a three-choice branching scenario on Screen 4).\n- **Data/evidence:** the observation that motivated it (pilot testers passed a recall quiz but still made the target error in the follow-up task).\n- **Warrant:** the principle that licenses moving from data to claim (decision practice with consequences builds judgment that recall does not).\n- **Alternatives and rebuttal:** the options weighed and why they lost (a quiz tested recall, not judgment; a five-branch tree exceeded the build window without adding distinct decision types).\n\nThe discipline behind this is sometimes called **design rationale capture** in the question-option-criteria tradition: for each design question, record the options considered and the criteria that decided among them. The reason it matters is **knowledge transfer and revisability**. A description lets a reviewer admire the design; a rationale lets them re-decide it when the situation changes. The most common failure is the **gulf between description and rationale**: listing components feels like documentation but records no reasoning, so the design cannot be defended or safely revised.\n\nMatch each sentence from the Screen 4 rationale to the Toulmin part it supplies, so you can spot which part a weak entry is missing.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Claim\",\"definition\":\"Use a three-choice branching scenario on Screen 4\"},{\"term\":\"Data / evidence\",\"definition\":\"Five of six pilot testers passed the recall quiz but still made the target error in the follow-up task\"},{\"term\":\"Warrant\",\"definition\":\"Decision practice with consequences builds judgment that recall items do not exercise\"},{\"term\":\"Rejected alternative\",\"definition\":\"A five-branch tree exceeded the two-week window without adding distinct decision types\"},{\"term\":\"Planned check\",\"definition\":\"Compare follow-up-task error rates before and after the change\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA thin entry describes contents:\n\n> Screen 4 has a branching scenario, a progress bar, and a Submit button.\n\nA rationale entry argues for the decision:\n\n> Claim: use a three-choice branching scenario on Screen 4. Evidence: five of six pilot testers passed a multiple-choice version but still made the target error afterward. Warrant: the goal is judgment under consequence, which recall items do not exercise. Rejected: a quiz (tested recall, not judgment) and a five-branch tree (exceeded the two-week window without adding distinct decision types).\n\nThe improvement is that a reviewer can now reconstruct why Screen 4 looks as it does, see what was traded off, and challenge a specific decision.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: From Description to Rationale\n\nHere is one entry in a design-rationale log for an onboarding module, revised so it functions as evidence rather than description.\n\n**Before (description).** \"Screen 4 has a branching scenario with three choices, a progress bar, and a Submit button. The colors match our brand.\"\n\nThe diagnosis: this lists what the screen contains. A reviewer learns nothing about why these decisions were made or whether they were reasoned.\n\n**After (rationale as evidence).**\n\n1. Decision: use a three-choice branching scenario on Screen 4.\n2. Motivation and evidence: pilot observation showed five of six testers passed a multiple-choice version while still making the target error in the follow-up task, so we needed decision practice with consequences, not recall.\n3. Alternatives weighed: a multiple-choice item (rejected, it tested recall not judgment) and a five-branch tree (rejected, the extra branches raised authoring time past our 2-week window without adding distinct decision types).\n4. Expected effect and check: testers should now encounter the consequence of a wrong choice; we will confirm by comparing follow-up-task error rates before and after.\n\n**Rejected suggestion.** An AI writing aid offered to \"polish the log into smoother prose.\" Rejected: smoother prose without the motivation, alternatives, and check would hide the very reasoning the log exists to expose.\n\nThe improvement is that a reviewer can now reconstruct why Screen 4 looks as it does, see what was traded off, and challenge a specific decision.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why a longer, more detailed description of Screen 4 is still not a rationale, no matter how thorough it is.\" answer=\"A description catalogs what the screen contains; adding more components only makes a longer catalog. A rationale is an argument: it needs a claim (the decision), the evidence that motivated it, the warrant linking evidence to claim, and at least one rejected alternative with its reason. Detail about contents supplies none of these argument parts, so a reviewer still cannot challenge or re-decide the choice. The missing ingredient is reasoning and the discarded options, not more description.\"></self-explain>\n\nWrite rationale as an argument with all four parts present, or it is description:\n\n1. **State the claim and the evidence together.** A decision with no motivating observation is an assertion; an observation with no decision is a note.\n2. **Make the warrant explicit.** Name the principle that licenses the move from evidence to claim, so a reviewer can challenge the principle, not just the taste.\n3. **Record at least one rejected alternative and why it lost.** A decision with no discarded options was never really a decision.\n\nIf an entry has no rejected alternative and no warrant, it is a description wearing the costume of a rationale.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that more detail equals rationale; a longer list of components is still a description. The second is letting an AI tool \"polish\" the log into smooth prose, which often deletes the rough but essential parts (the alternatives, the warrant, the planned check) because they read awkwardly.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An entry reads: Screen 4 uses a branching scenario because branching scenarios are an engaging modern format. Which Toulmin part is doing the work, and what is missing?\" explanation=\"This is a claim attached to a generic appeal, not a warrant grounded in the goal or in evidence. It cites no motivating data (the pilot error), no warrant tied to building judgment, and no rejected alternative. It is description wearing the costume of a rationale.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is a complete rationale because it gives a reason\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is a claim with no motivating evidence, no goal-tied warrant, and no rejected alternative\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is strong because engagement justifies the format\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n The third is treating rationale as a post-hoc justification written after the design is locked; captured after the fact, it tends to rationalize rather than record, and it cannot inform the decision it claims to explain.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the Screen 4 description and interrogate it like a skeptic. \"Branching scenario, three choices\" tells you the claim but hides the argument, so ask: what evidence motivated branching over a quiz? The pilot data (passed recall, still erred in practice) supplies it. Ask: what principle connects that data to the choice? The warrant that judgment-under-consequence is not built by recall. Ask: what else was on the table? A quiz and a five-branch tree, each rejected for a stated reason. Ask: how will we know it worked? A planned before-and-after comparison of follow-up error rates. Each question converts a piece of description into a piece of argument, and only when all four are present can a reviewer attack the design at a specific joint rather than shrug at a feature list. That is the difference between documentation that looks complete and evidence that is defensible.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every design statement and ask \"is this a claim, evidence, a warrant, or a rejected alternative, or is it just a description?\" A strong reader can take a paragraph of design notes and label which Toulmin part each sentence supplies, then name what is missing. Keep a two-column note: the statement on the left, the argument part it provides (or \"description, no argument\") on the right.\n\nA strong reader of design rationale as evidence can answer four questions:\n\n1. What is the claim, and what evidence motivates it?\n2. What warrant licenses moving from the evidence to the claim?\n3. Which alternatives were weighed, and why did they lose?\n4. How will we check whether the decision worked?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe log is strong when each entry contains a claim, motivating evidence, an explicit warrant, at least one rejected alternative with a reason, and a planned check. A component list fails; a four-part argument with a verification plan passes. If an entry has no rejected alternative or no warrant, mark the gap and ask BigAL to surface the discarded options and the licensing principle rather than to smooth the prose.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I argued for the branching scenario from pilot error data under a judgment-not-recall warrant, recorded the rejected quiz and five-branch alternatives, and set an error-rate check. I rejected the AI's prose-polishing because it would have erased the alternatives and the warrant.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the rationale into a high-stakes context: a regulated compliance course where an auditor will later ask why each instructional decision was made. Now the rationale is not just good practice; it is the legal record. The four-part structure does not change, but the standard for evidence and the planned check tighten, because \"pilot observation\" may need to become documented validation. The same description-versus-rationale gulf that hurt a low-stakes onboarding module becomes a liability here: a feature list cannot survive an audit, while a claim-evidence-warrant-alternatives entry can. A designer who captured rationale as argument can hand the auditor a defensible trail; one who only described screens has nothing to show.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the design-rationale log entry packet (claim, evidence, warrant, rejected alternatives, planned check)](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-07-design-rationale-as-evidence-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"design-rationale log entry with claim, evidence, warrant, rejected alternatives, and planned check\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the design-rationale log entry with claim, evidence, warrant, rejected alternatives, and planned check.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the design-rationale log entry with claim, evidence, warrant, rejected alternatives, and planned check for AIL 606 01.07. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that each rationale entry contains a claim, motivating evidence, an explicit warrant, at least one rejected alternative with its reason, and a planned check, plus any suggestion you rejected and why.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect design rationale as an argument to the evidence-based revision it enables and the bounded role AI plays when it offers to polish the log.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence","stem":"Which statement is a design rationale rather than a design description?","options":["The intro screen has a blue header, a title, and a Start button.","We replaced the autoplay video with a Start button because pilot testers skipped ahead, and learner-controlled pacing reduced that off-task behavior.","The module contains eight screens and a quiz.","The font is Arial at 16 points throughout."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A rationale states the decision, the problem or evidence that motivated it, and the expected effect. The others merely describe what the artifact contains.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_is_description","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence","stem":"A rationale reads: 'We added the diagram because it makes the page more engaging and looks better.' Why is this not yet defensible evidence?","options":["Because diagrams should never be added to a page.","Because it appeals to taste rather than to a learning principle or observed evidence, so a reviewer cannot evaluate or challenge the claim.","Because engagement is irrelevant to instructional design.","Because a rationale must always be at least one page long."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Defensible rationale grounds the decision in a principle or evidence a reviewer can test. 'Looks better' is a preference, not inspectable evidence.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_is_preference","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence","stem":"Why does a strong design rationale record the alternatives considered and rejected, not only the final choice?","options":["To make the document longer for grading.","Because showing what was weighed and why one option was rejected lets a reviewer judge whether the decision was reasoned rather than arbitrary.","Because rejected options must always be implemented later.","Because the final choice is never the important part."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Recording considered-and-rejected alternatives exposes the reasoning, turning the rationale into auditable evidence of judgment rather than an after-the-fact justification.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_only_final_choice","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_design_rationale_as_evidence"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-02","ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_design_rationale_as_evidence_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","stem":"A reviewer should be able to do which of the following after reading a strong design rationale?","options":["Admire the visual polish of the final product.","Reconstruct why each major decision was made, what evidence supported it, and what was traded off, then challenge a specific decision.","Count the number of screens in the module.","Identify the designer's favorite color scheme."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Rationale as evidence makes reasoning inspectable: a reviewer can trace, evaluate, and contest specific decisions, which is the purpose of documenting it.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_is_description","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-01-07-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_is_description","pattern":"rationale_as_description","description":"Confusing a design rationale with a description of what the artifact contains, listing features instead of giving reasons.","trigger":"learner writes what the screen has rather than why each decision was made","feedback":"A rationale states the decision, the motivating problem or evidence, and the expected effect. Replace 'the screen has X' with 'we chose X because the evidence showed Y.'","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_is_preference","pattern":"rationale_as_preference","description":"Grounding a rationale in taste or aesthetics ('looks better', 'more engaging') rather than a principle or observed evidence a reviewer can test.","trigger":"learner justifies a decision by personal preference","feedback":"Ground each decision in a learning principle or pilot evidence so a reviewer can evaluate and challenge it. Preference is not evidence.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_1_7_rationale_only_final_choice","pattern":"rationale_omits_alternatives","description":"Recording only the final decision and omitting the alternatives that were considered and rejected.","trigger":"learner documents the final choice with no weighed alternatives","feedback":"Show the alternatives you considered and why you rejected them. The reasoning, not just the outcome, is what makes the rationale auditable evidence.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d05d8f0055e29935852b395455a592b295f61e0f41297c47d78c5c4129546b41","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}